326 0 obj
<<
/Title (A guest house earns money only when its rooms are occupied, and a room left empty on a given night cannot be sold again for that night. If all seven rooms were occupied, the guest house would earn Rs. 6,800 a night, from three AC rooms at Rs. 1,200 and four non-AC rooms at Rs. 800, so every empty room matters. The problems listed above arise from a mix of internal and external causes.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 276 0 R
/Next 381 0 R
>>
endobj
327 0 obj
<<
/Title (These causes work together, because seasonal swings expose the lack of records and the dependence on walk-ins leaves little room to plan ahead. The project will therefore study how these problems appear in the guest house's own data and which of them can be reduced.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 276 0 R
/Prev 381 0 R
>>
endobj
328 0 obj
<<
/Title (The project will follow four steps: understand the business, collect the data, clean and analyse it, and draw recommendations. Two to three meetings with the owner, with field notes of each, will confirm the problem, because the owner knows the seasons, the guests and the earlier efforts better than any outside source. Occupancy trend analysis by month and weekday, with a comparison of the festive, winter and other months, will show when rooms remain empty. Revenue analysis by room type will show how much the AC and non-AC rooms earn. A comparison of tariff, occupancy and revenue per available room will show whether the current rates suit each season. Grouping guests by arrival day and length of stay will show how walk-in guests behave. Each method matches one problem statement.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 277 0 R
>>
endobj
329 0 obj
<<
/Title (5.2 Data collection and justification)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 237 0 R
/Prev 277 0 R
/First 382 0 R
/Last 382 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 278 0 R
>>
endobj
330 0 obj
<<
/Title (Excel will be used for data entry, quick checks and pivot tables, because the owner can open and understand it easily. Python, with pandas and matplotlib, will be used for cleaning, grouping, descriptive statistics and charts, because it handles large records quickly and makes the work repeatable. Cleaning will remove duplicates, fix date and tariff errors, and flag missing values, and the cleaned data will be cross-checked against the owner's bills. Descriptive statistics such as the mean, median and range will summarise occupancy and tariffs, and correlation will test the link between tariff and occupancy. Every chart will have a title, labelled axes and a stated reason for the chosen chart type.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 278 0 R
>>
endobj
